        <h2>What makes HubSpot lead routing work?</h2>
        <p class="guide-answer">Lead routing works when every qualified lead gets a clear owner, a clear next step, and a measurable response time. In HubSpot that means agreed lifecycle definitions, a documented rule order, SLA timers that detect a miss, and a recovery queue for leads that slip through. Automation enforces the rules; it does not replace them.</p>
        <p class="intro">This guide connects lifecycle design, fit and intent signals, territory rules, SLAs, and escalation paths into one operating system. It is the structure we build on <a href="/solutions/revops/">our HubSpot RevOps projects</a>.</p>

        <section id="moments">
          <h2>Which lifecycle moments should trigger routing?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Route at the moments where ownership changes: when a lead becomes marketing qualified, when sales accepts it, and when it is recycled. Lifecycle stage records the relationship; lead status records the working state. Keeping them separate lets marketing, sales, and service report on the same journey without overwriting each other.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Define the entry and exit criteria for each lifecycle stage.</li>
            <li>Use lead status for operational follow-up: new, working, connected, qualified, unqualified, or recycled.</li>
            <li>Document who owns the record at each handoff.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>If your stages are not defined yet, start with <a href="/resources/hubspot-lifecycle-stages-explained/">HubSpot lifecycle stages explained</a>, then come back to routing.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="ready-to-route">
          <h2>How do you know a record is ready to route?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">A record is ready when the fields routing depends on are filled, consent is recorded, and it has been checked against existing contacts and companies. Records that fail those checks go to a nurture or data-fix path instead of a rep, so sales never receives a lead it cannot act on.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Required routing fields present: country or region, company size, product interest.</li>
            <li>Duplicate check against existing contacts and the company's current owner.</li>
            <li>Incomplete records sent to enrichment or nurture, not to a rep's queue.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="rule-order">
          <h2>In what order should routing rules run?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Run the rules that must never be broken first, then the ones that balance workload. A typical order is existing account owner, named accounts and territory, product and language, customer status, and finally round-robin among available reps. Every branch ends in a named fallback owner.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Rule</th><th>Why it runs here</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>1</td><td>Existing owner of the contact or company</td><td>Keeps one owner per account and avoids two reps calling the same buyer.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2</td><td>Named accounts and territory</td><td>Contractual or strategic ownership that must not be overridden.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3</td><td>Product, language, or segment</td><td>Sends the lead to someone who can actually sell what was asked for.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>4</td><td>Customer status</td><td>Existing customers go to account management, not new business.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>5</td><td>Round-robin with capacity</td><td>Spreads the remainder fairly across available reps.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>6</td><td>Fallback owner or queue</td><td>Guarantees no lead ends the workflow without an owner.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="fit-intent">
          <h2>How do fit and intent change routing?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Fit says whether the buyer matches your ideal customer; intent says whether they are ready to talk. Combine both to set priority, not ownership. A high-fit lead requesting a demo goes to the front of the queue with a short response target, while low-intent leads can wait or go to nurture.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Use intent signals such as demo requests, high-value page visits, and repeated engagement.</li>
            <li>Balance round-robin assignment with rep capacity and out-of-office coverage.</li>
            <li>Review scoring thresholds against closed-won data every quarter.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="sla">
          <h2>How do you set up SLA timers in HubSpot?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Stamp the assignment time on the record, stamp the first meaningful response, and run a workflow that checks the gap. When the response target is close to expiring, remind the owner; when it expires, escalate to a manager or reassign. Report on both timestamps so the SLA is measured, not assumed.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Create date properties for "assigned at" and "first response at", set by workflow.</li>
            <li>Set response targets by lead type and priority, agreed with sales leadership.</li>
            <li>Remind the owner before the target expires; escalate after it does.</li>
            <li>Track speed and quality together, so automation does not reward low-quality handoffs.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Leads sitting unassigned?</strong> We design and build routing and SLAs inside your portal. <button type="button" class="text-link js-open-hs-form">Book a free consultation</button></p></div>
        </section>

        <section id="exceptions">
          <h2>What happens when routing fails?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Every routing system has exceptions: records with no match, owners who are away, and leads nobody works. Send them to a no-owner and no-response queue that a named person reviews every business day. The queue is how you find the gaps in your rules and fix them.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Escalate unworked records before the SLA expires.</li>
            <li>Create a no-owner and no-response view reviewed every business day.</li>
            <li>Log the reason each record landed there, and fix the rule that caused it.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="recycled">
          <h2>How should recycled leads flow back?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">When sales marks a lead as not ready, set lead status to recycled, record the reason, clear the owner or keep it by agreement, and move the record into a nurture path with a review date. If the lead re-engages, it re-enters routing at the top with its history intact.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Recycled reason captured as a dropdown, not free text, so it can be reported on.</li>
            <li>Lifecycle stage moved back only by a documented rule, because HubSpot moves stages forward by default.</li>
            <li>Re-engagement triggers send the lead back through the same rule order.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

